 ],
 "literal": "Look, O bard! It is of noble character indeed! / In the fresh white moonlight spread in the evening, / on the bed of fragrant flowers on the short-legged cot, / like a sleeping elephant, he sighed, and with desire, / the victorious hero embraced his son; / the mother of the son embraced his back indeed.",
 "translation": "\"Look, O bard! How noble it is! In the fresh white moonlight that spreads in the evening, on a short-legged cot strewn with fragrant flowers, the victorious hero, sighing like a sleeping elephant, embraced his son with deep love. And the mother of his son, in turn, embraced him from behind.\"",
 "modern": "பாணனே, பார்! இது எவ்வளவு பண்பான செயல்! மாலை நேரத்தில் பரவிய புதிய வெண்மையான நிலவொளியில், குட்டையான கால்களைக் கொண்ட கட்டிலில் பரப்பப்பட்ட நறுமணமிக்க மலர்ப்படுக்கையில், உறங்கும் யானையைப் போலப் பெருமூச்சு விட்டபடி, தன் மகன் மீது கொண்ட அன்பினால் அந்த வெற்றியாளன் தன் மகனைத் தழுவிக்கொண்டான். உடனே, அச்சிறுவனின் தாயான தலைவி, அவனது முதுகைக் கட்டியணைத்துக் கொண்டாள்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Marutam (agricultural) landscape, which deals with infidelity and reconciliation. The speaker is the heroine's friend (tōḻi), addressing the bard (pāṇan) who has come as a messenger from the hero. She describes a beautiful domestic scene that occurred in the evening under the soft moonlight. The hero, returning home, lovingly embraced his sleeping son on their flower-strewn bed, sighing deeply like a resting elephant. Seeing this display of paternal love, the heroine forgot her anger over his trysts with courtesans and embraced him from behind. By sharing this, the friend indicates to the bard that the heroine has forgiven the hero, and he is welcome to return.",
